    about=["On March 18, 1766, Parliament repealed the Stamp Act. In the colonies there were bells, bonfires, and toasts to the king. That same day, the same Parliament passed this second, much shorter law, which almost no one celebrated because almost no one paid attention to it.",
           "Its content fits in one sentence: Parliament drops the tax and keeps the whole right to impose it. Not only the right to tax: the right to make laws for America <em>in all cases whatsoever</em>, with no exception stated.",
           "That is why it is paired with Document 9. If you study only the repeal, 1766 looks like a happy ending and the Revolution ten years later has no explanation. Read the two laws of the same day and you see something else: the immediate conflict (the money) was settled, and the underlying conflict (the power) was left untouched. It is, in pure form, the difference between an immediate cause and a structural cause that Lesson 6.1 works on."],
    passages_intro="Two passages are quoted word for word in Lesson 6.1. The first, because its formula comes back ten years later with the subject reversed. The second, because it cancels Document 9, and any text like it, in advance.",
